WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.440
Speculative decoding can turn
one forward pass into four tokens.

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.960
It works because checking a
guess costs far less than making one.

3
00:00:08.714 --> 00:00:11.114
Recall that decode is memory-bound.

4
00:00:11.194 --> 00:00:15.784
Each pass reads all the weights to
produce one token, while compute sits idle.

5
00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:18.514
Speculation puts that
idle compute to work.

6
00:00:19.212 --> 00:00:24.372
A speculator, such as a small
draft model, proposes draft tokens.

7
00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:29.572
The target model is the model you are
accelerating, and only its output counts.

8
00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.340
The text so far reads 'Once upon a'.

9
00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.680
The speculator drafts five tokens:
'time', a comma, 'there', 'lived', and 'a'.

10
00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:43.726
The target model checks all five
drafts in one forward pass, in parallel.

11
00:00:43.816 --> 00:00:46.686
It accepts 'time', the comma, and 'there'.

12
00:00:46.776 --> 00:00:49.846
It rejects 'lived', because
it would have written 'was'.

13
00:00:49.936 --> 00:00:53.606
It discards the final 'a',
because it follows a rejection.

14
00:00:53.696 --> 00:00:56.356
Then it adds its own token, 'was'.

15
00:00:57.019 --> 00:01:00.269
One forward pass produced
four tokens instead of one.

16
00:01:00.279 --> 00:01:05.269
If every draft fails, the pass still
yields one token, the same as ordinary decode.

17
00:01:05.935 --> 00:01:07.505
Pause and trace.

18
00:01:07.505 --> 00:01:10.435
A speculator drafts six tokens.

19
00:01:10.435 --> 00:01:13.295
The target accepts the
first two and rejects the third.

20
00:01:13.455 --> 00:01:17.065
How many drafts are discarded, and
how many tokens does the pass produce?
